[vset VERSION 0.1]
[manpage_begin map::display n [vset VERSION]]
[category {Map Display and Supporting Utilities}][keywords map]
[keywords {map display} {display, map}]
[moddesc   {Map display support}]
[titledesc {Map Display Widget}]
[require Tcl 8.6-]
[require Tk  8.6-]
[require canvas::sqmap]
[require canvas::zoom]
[require crosshair]
[require debug]
[require debug::caller]
[require snit]
[require widget::scrolledwindow]
[require map::display [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]

This package provides a map display widget with basic behaviour: panning, centering, and zooming.

The map tiles are fetched from a configurable tile provider like [package map::provider::osm], or
anything API compatible, see section [sectref {Tile provider}].

The widget is extensible with attachable engines providing additional behaviours, like the display
of geo/* resources, and/or the editing of geo/* resources, see section
[sectref {Attaching behaviours}].

[para] The widget uses the conventions of package [package map::slippy] for locations and zoom
levels:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [term geo]]
A geographical location, a 2-element list of latitude and longitude, in degrees.

[def [term geobox]]
A geographical box, a 4-element list of the minimal latitude and longitude, and the maximal
latitude and longitude, in this order.

[def [term {zoom level}]]
An integer between [const 0] and the number of levels supported by the provider minus one, see
method [method levels]. At zoom level [var z] the whole world is a square of
[const 256]*2**[var z] pixels.

[def [term point]]
A pixel location at a given zoom level, a 2-element list of x and y coordinates. These are also
the coordinates on the canvas returned by method [method canvas].

[def [term pointbox]]
A box of pixel locations at a given zoom level, a 4-element list of the minimal x and y, and the
maximal x and y coordinates, in this order.
[list_end]

[section {Class API}]

[list_begin definitions]
[call [cmd ::map::display] [arg pathName] [opt "[arg option] [arg value]..."]]
[call [cmd {::map display}] [arg pathName] [opt "[arg option] [arg value]..."]]

This command creates a new map display widget named [arg pathName], configured with the specified
options, and returns the [arg pathName].

The option [option -provider] is required.

[para] The widget consists of a label at the top showing the geographical location under the mouse
pointer, and the map itself, with a zoom control in its top left corner.
[list_end]

[section Options]

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -provider [arg command-prefix]]

The tile provider used to retrieve the map tiles, see section [sectref {Tile provider}].

This option is required and cannot be set to the empty string.

The command prefix is not invoked at the global level, so its command name should be fully
qualified, like the names returned by the constructors of [package snit] types.

[para] The tile size and the number of zoom levels are taken from the provider when the widget is
created. When the option is changed afterwards, only the tiles requested from then on come from the
new provider.

[opt_def -on-view-change [arg command-prefix]]

A command prefix invoked when the map has moved to a new location: after the map has been moved by
method [method center] or by option [option -initial-geo], after a zoom change, after the map
has been re-centered because its size changed or it was locked, and after the user finished
panning the map.

[para] It is invoked at the global level with the three elements of the result of method
[method view] (zoom level, [term pointbox], and [term geobox] of the visible part of the map) as
additional arguments. Its result is ignored.

[para] The default is the empty string, i.e. no reporting.

[para] Attached engines like [package map::box::map-display] use this option to track the
visible part of the map, see section [sectref {Attaching behaviours}].

[opt_def -initial-geo [arg geo]]

The [term geo] location to show at the center of the map after the widget was created.

If not specified (the default) the map starts at its top left corner.

This option can only be set at creation time.

[opt_def -initial-zoom [arg zoom]]

The initial zoom level. The default is [const 12].

This option can only be set at creation time.

[opt_def -locked [arg boolean]]

When true the user cannot pan the map. The center of the map can then only be changed by the
method [method center]; it is kept when the widget is resized.

When the option is set to true while the widget exists, the map moves back to the last center set
by the method [method center], the option [option -initial-geo], or a finished panning operation.

Zooming is not affected by this option.

The default is [const 0].

[opt_def -center-mark [arg image]]

The name of an image to show at the center of the map. The empty string (the default) shows no
mark.
[list_end]

[section Methods]

[list_begin definitions]
[call [arg pathName] [method levels]]

This method returns the number of zoom levels supported by the tile provider. The valid zoom
levels are [const 0] to this number minus one.

[call [arg pathName] [method zoom-to] [arg zoom]]

This method sets the zoom level of the map to [arg zoom], like the zoom control does.

Invalid zoom levels are ignored.

The result of the method is the empty string.

[call [arg pathName] [method center] [arg geo] [opt [arg zoom]]]

This method moves the map so that the [term geo] location is shown at its center, and when the
[arg zoom] level is specified and valid, switches to that level as well.

The move is done asynchronously, after a short delay; the option [option -on-view-change]
reports it when done.

This is the only way to move a locked map, see option [option -locked].

The result of the method is the empty string.

[call [arg pathName] [method canvas]]

This method returns the path of the canvas widget (a [package canvas::sqmap]) showing the map.

The canvas coordinates are the [term point] coordinates for the current zoom level.

Attached engines use it to place their items.

[call [arg pathName] [method at]]

This method returns the [term geo] location under the mouse pointer, i.e. the location of the
crosshair, as last seen while the pointer was over the map. The latitude and longitude are
rounded to 6 decimal places.

[call [arg pathName] [method view]]

This method returns a 3-element list describing the visible part of the map: the current zoom
level, the visible part as a [term pointbox], and the visible part as a [term geobox].

[list_end]

[section Interaction]

[list_begin itemized]
[item] Dragging the map with mouse button 3 pressed pans the map.
[item] Clicking with mouse button 3 while the [const Shift] key is pressed centers the map at the
       clicked location.
[item] The mouse wheel, or the zoom control, zooms the map in and out. When zooming in, the
       location under the mouse pointer becomes the new center of the map; when zooming out the
       center is kept.
[item] The label above the map shows the [term geo] location under the mouse pointer.
[list_end]

Panning and centering by click are disabled when the map is locked, see option [option -locked].

[section {Tile provider}]

A tile provider is a command prefix, typically a [package snit] object like those created by
[package map::provider::osm], supporting the methods listed below.

The widget assumes the standard tile size of [package map::slippy] ([const 256] pixels) for its
geographical computations, so the provider has to deliver tiles of that size.

[list_begin definitions]
[call [arg provider] [method levels]]

This method has to return the number of zoom levels supported by the provider.

[call [arg provider] [method tilewidth]]
[call [arg provider] [method tileheight]]

These methods have to return the width and the height of the tiles, in pixels.

[call [arg provider] [method get] [arg tile] [arg donecmd]]

This method is invoked to request the image of a [arg tile], a 3-element list of zoom level, row,
and column, in this order.

The result of the method is ignored.

The provider has to invoke the command prefix [arg donecmd], immediately or later, in one of the
following forms:

[example {
    {*}$donecmd set   $tile $image  ;# The image for the tile is available
    {*}$donecmd unset $tile         ;# There is no image for the tile
}]

where [arg tile] is the requested tile and [arg image] the name of a Tk image of the tile.
[list_end]

[section {Attaching behaviours}]

The behaviour of the widget is extended by engines attached to it, like
[package map::area::map-display], [package map::box::map-display],
[package map::point::map-display], [package map::track::map-display], [package map::box::entry],
[package map::track::entry], and [package map::mark].

An engine is an object created with the map widget as an argument. It uses only the public API of
the widget: the canvas returned by method [method canvas] for drawing its items and binding its
events, the methods [method view], [method levels], [method center], and [method at] to query and
change the map, and the option [option -on-view-change] to track the visible part of the map.

[para] To share the option [option -on-view-change] the engines chain their callbacks: on
creation an engine saves the current value of the option, replaces it with its own callback, and
forwards every report to the saved command prefix; on destruction it restores the saved value.

Hence a command prefix set before creating the engines keeps working, and when engines are
destroyed, this must happen in the reverse order of their creation.

[section EXAMPLE]

A map using a provider which creates its tiles locally, showing each tile as an outlined white
square:

[example {
package require map::display

proc localtiles {method args} {
    switch -exact -- $method {
        levels     { return 6 }
        tilewidth  -
        tileheight { return 256 }
        get {
            lassign $args tile donecmd
            set image [image create photo -width 256 -height 256]
            $image put black -to 0 0 256 256
            $image put white -to 1 1 255 255
            # Report the image asynchronously, as a real provider would.
            after idle [list {*}$donecmd set $tile $image]
        }
    }
}

proc show {zoom pointbox geobox} {
    puts "zoom $zoom, showing $geobox"
}

map display .map -provider ::localtiles -on-view-change show \
    -initial-geo {48.137 11.575} -initial-zoom 4
pack .map -fill both -expand 1
}]

